window.PLD=window.PLD||{};PLD["R-tx-2022-01-18"]={"AD-001-22":"Resolution No. AD-001-22\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nAUTHOmZATION FOR REAL PROPERTY TAX\nREFUNDS/CORRECTIONS - 2022\nWHEREAS, New York State Real Property Tax Law, sections 554 and section 556, provides for an administrative correction of error process that assists property owners in obtaining refunds and/or corrections for real property tax payments or billings, and\nWHEREAS, certain clerical errors, unlawful entries and errors in essential fact, as defined in the New York State Real Property Tax Law, are permitted to be corrected by a taxing jurisdiction based upon the recommendation of the Director of Real Property Tax Services, and\nWHEREAS, the taxing jurisdiction can delegate, by resolution, on an annual basis, to an official who is empowered to authorize payment of bills without prior audit, the authority to perform the duties of the tax levying body in regard to Real Property Tax Law, Sections 554 and 556, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Treasurer is hereby delegated the authority to perform the duties of the tax levying body during 2022 when the Real Property Tax Director s recommended refund and/or correction is twenty five hundred dollars or less, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that on or before the fifteenth day of each month, the Niagara County Treasurer shall submit a report to the Niagara County Legislature of the refunds and/or corrections processed during the preceding month, containing the name of each recipient, the location of the property, and the amount of the refund.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","AD-002-22":"Resolution No. AD-002-22\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nEXTENSION OF CONTRACT WITH EASTERN NIAGARA HO SPITALAVE STERN NEW YORK\nOCCUPATIONAL MEDICINE, P.C.\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has had a contractual agreement with Eastern Niagara Hospital/Westem New York Occupational Medicine, P.C., to provide certain occupational medicine professional services for the County of Niagara, and\nWHEREAS, the contract with Eastern Niagara Hospital/Westem New York Occupational Medicine, P.C. expired on December 31, 2021, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County issued RFP 2021-49 seeking competitive bids for professional services, and\nWHEREAS, as a result of the RFP 2021-49, the County of Niagara desires to continue this contractual relationship and enter into a new contract term with Eastern Niagara Hospital/Westem New York Occupational Medicine, P.C. for the period of January 1, 2022 through December 31, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the contract, the County Attorney will review the contract for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature is hereby authorized to execute said contract on behalf of Niagara County.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","AD-003-22":"Resolution No. AD-003-22\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY BOARD OF ELECTIONS\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - GRANT ACCEPTANCE\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Board of Elections Office has been awarded a grant in the amount of $119,055.71 for the period of December 21, 2019 through December 31, 2023 from the New York State Board of Elections to implement cybersecurity and the various accompanying services and/or accessories required, and\nWHEREAS, the grant will allow the Niagara County Board of Elections Office to purchase equipment and or services regarding cybersecurity, and\nWHEREAS, the 2022 budget will need to be modified to allow for the spending of this grant, now therefore, be it\nRESOLVED that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the agreement and be it further\nRESOLVED that the 2022 budget be modified as follows:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.14.1450.000 44089.01 Help America Vote Act $29,056.71\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.14.1450.000 74500.01 Contractual Expense $29,056.71\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CS-001-22":"Resolution No. CS-001-22\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY DEPARTMENT OF MENTAL HEALTH & SUBSTANCE ABUSE SVCS\nPROGRAM RESTRUCTURE - ABOLISH LICENSED CLINICIAN POSITION, ABOLISH\nREGISTERED NURSE POSITION/ CREATE AND FILL STAFF SOCIAL WORKER AND\nLICENSED PRACTICAL NURSE POSITIONS / BUDGET MODIFICATION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Mental Health & Substance Abuse Services (NCDMH) is committed to providing exceptional treatment and integrated care services to Niagara County residents in need, and\nWHEREAS, a Niagara County Department of Mental Health & Substance Abuse Services program restructure is necessary to address organizational and client needs, and\nWHEREAS, creating a Staff Social Worker position will provide leadership, guidance, and support to Licensed Clinicians in the Assisted Outpatient Treatment program, and support clinical decision making in light of increasing complexity of high-risk clients,\nWHEREAS, the reorganization of our nursing services is necessary to respond to workforce shortage concerns, RN recruitment challenges, and address the need to expand medical care management for clinic clients, and\nWHEREAS, NCDMH is committed to and has demonstrated operating in a responsible fiscal manner, and\nWHEREAS, the Staff Social Worker position is supported 100% through State Aid, and the LPN position is supported 100% through generated revenue, both positions at no cost to the County, and\nWHEREAS, these changes will result in an overall cost savings to the Department; now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that one Licensed Clinician (vacant as of June 3, 2021), Grade 11, Step 1, position number MHD300100.10818, salary range $51,870.00 - $60,624.20, and one Registered Nurse (RN) (vacant as of October 10, 2021), Grade 12, Step 1, position number MHD300100.13633, salary range $55,892.20 $65,520.00 be abolished, effective January 24,2022,and be it further\nRESOLVED, that one Staff Social Worker, Grade 13, Step 1, position number, MHD300100.XXXXX, salary range $60,496.80 - $70,470.40, and one Licensed Practical Nurse (LPN), Grade 8, Step 1, position number, MHD300100.XXXXX, salary range $40,513.20 - $47,429.20 be created within the Niagara County Department of Mental Health, and filled effective January 24, 2022, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following transfer of position funds in the 2022 budget be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.21.4310.000.43490.05 Reinvestment Programs $10,268\nDECREASE REVENUE: .\nA.21.4310.000.41620.00 Mental HealthFees $18,307\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.21.4310.000.71010.XXXXX  Positions (Staff Social Worker)  $60,497\nA.21.4310.000.71010.XXXXX  Positions (LPN)  $40,513\nA.21.4310.000.78100.00  Retirement Expense  $ 8,838\nA.21.4310.000.78200.00  FICA  $ 7,727\nA.21.4310.000.78300.00  Worker's Comp  $ 2,656\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.21.4310.000.71010.00.10818  Positions (Licensed Clinician)  $51,870\nA.21.4310.000.71010.10633  Positions (RN)  $55,892\nA.21.4310.000.78100.00  Retirement Expense  $ 9,430\nA.21.4310.000.78200.00  FICA  $ 8,244\nA.21.4310.000.78300.00  Worker s Comp  $ 2,834\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CS-002-22":"Resolution No. CS-002-22\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nABOLISH E&T COORDINATOR/CREATE & FILL E&T TRAINING COUNSELOR POSITION-\nEMPLOYMENT & TRAINING\nWHEREAS, the duties of Employment & Training Coordinator do not meet the present needs of Employment & Training) and\nWHEREAS, the duties of Employment & Training Counselor represent the duties necessary for the daily operations of Employment & Training, and\nWHEREAS, the flexibility and scope of duties of Employment & Training Counselor better serves Employment & Training needs, and\nWHEREAS, the change reflects no increase to the County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that effective January 31, 2022 Employment & Training Coordinator (Position #13624), CSEA Job Grade 9 at $45,464 per year be abolished, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that effective January 31, 2022 the position of Employment & Training Counselor, CSEA Job Grade 9, Step 1 at $43,989 be created and filled.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent ~ Elder.","CS-003-22":"Resolution No. CS-003-22\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - CREATE TEMPORARY POSITION\nWHEREAS, an Office for the Aging employee has been on an extended medical leave of absence and it is unknown at this time when the incumbent will return to work, and\nWHEREAS, the duties of this position involve providing critical health related services to OFA clients, and\nWHEREAS, the department is seeking a temporary replacement in this position to continue to meet its program services delivery requirements, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that a temporary FT Van Driver position be created and filled immediately at the designated AFSCME hourly rate for this position at step 5 of $14.05 per hour, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the temporary position will be abolished upon the return of the current incumbent, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated.\nDECREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.24.7610.702 71030.01 Part Time Expense $20,380\nPosition ID 3709 $17,989\nPosition ID 1349 $ 2,391\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.24.6772.000 71010.00 Positions Expense $ 10,959\nA.24.6772.000 78100.00 Retirement Expense 959\nA.24.6772.000 78400.01 Insurance, Health Active Expense 6,089\nA.24.6772.000 78400.05 Insurance, Health HRA Employer Contribution 850\nA.24.6772.000 78800.00 Flex 125 Employer Contribution Expense 403\nA.24.6772.000 78200.00 FICA Expense . 838\nA.24.6772.000 78300.00 Worker's Compensation 282\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-001-22":"Resolution No. CSS-001-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nACCEPT MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT AND INSURANCE FRAUD PREVENTION GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office was notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services that it has been awarded $20,000 under the Motor Vehicle Theft and Insurance Fraud Prevention Program, and\nWHEREAS, the Motor Vehicle Theft and Insurance Fraud Prevention Grant has been awarded to the Niagara County Sheriffs Office for many years, and\nWHEREAS, the performance period for this grant is January 1 , 2022 through December 3 1,2022,and\nWHEREAS, the grant is used to coordinate efforts to reduce the incidents of motor vehicle theft and motor vehicle insurance fraud County-wide; this partnership provides an integrated means to prevent and deter motor vehicle theft and insurance fraud related crime through the expanded use of crime data, crime analysts, technology and information sharing, and\nWHEREAS, the funds are included in the 2022 budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute this grant.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent ~- Elder.","CSS-002-22":"Resolution No. CSS-002-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE BUDGET MODIFICATION\nEXTENSION OF RESIDENTIAL SUBSTANCE ABUSE TREATMENT GRANT\nWHEREAS, on May 12, 2020 the Niagara County Legislature accepted a grant from the New York State Department of Criminal Justice Services (resolution #CSS-022\"20) in the amount of $50,000 for a third year to support a Residential Substance Abuse Treatment (RSAT) Cognitive-Behavioral Intervention program within the Niagara County Jail for the period of January 1, 2020 through December 31, 2020, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature accepted an extension of the Residential Substance Abuse Treatment (RSAT) Cognitive-Behavioral Intervention Program through September 30, 2021 in Resolution #CSS-030-21 on March 23, 2021, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office been notified by the New York State Department of Criminal Justice Services that the performance period for the grant has been extended through July 3 1,2022,and\nWHEREAS, it is well known that jails and prisons are dealing with incarcerated individuals who have substance abuse and addiction problems, and\nWHEREAS, it is desirable to have a program which addresses the substance abuse and addiction issues with the goal of changing the incarcerated individuals' addiction behaviors to improve their lives and reduce recidivism, and\nWHEREAS, this grant will allow the Niagara County Jail to continue this program which was started in 2018 by contracting with an outside agency to provide a substance abuse treatment cognitive-behavioral intervention program, and\nWHEREAS, the 2022 budget needs to be amended to accept and expend this grant extension, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that prior to the execution of the grant, the County Attorney will review the grant for approval to legal form, language and compliance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute this grant extension, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.17.3150.000.44089.00 Federal Aid, Other Revenue $25,666\nLNTCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3150.000.74400.09 Payments Other Agencies $25,666\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-003-22":"Resolution No. CSS-003-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - EXTENSION OF FY18 STATEWIDE INTEROPERABLE\nCOMMUNICATIONS FORMULA GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature accepted a grant from the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services in the amount of $669,712.00 to the Niagara County Sheriffs Office for the grant period of January 1, 2019 through December 31,2020 in Resolution #CSS-002-19 on January 15, 2019, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services has extended the performance period of this grant until December 31, 2022, and\nWHEREAS, this grant allows the Sheriffs Office to continue the improvement of the interoperable communications network which will have the benefit to the residents of Niagara County of improved reliability of communication for their safety and protection, and\nWHEREAS, an improved interoperable communications network will also enhance interoperable communications with other counties and agencies when participating in Mutual Aid emergencies, and\nWHEREAS, the 2022 budget will need to be modified to allow for the spending of the balance of this grant,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be and hereby is authorized to execute the extension of this grant through December 31,2022, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000.43305.02 State Aid, Civil Defense Homeland Security $226,062\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000.72100.15 Machinery and Equipment Communications Equipment $226,062\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-004-22":"Resolution No. CSS-004-22\nFrom: Community Safety &. Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nEXTENSION OF FY19 STATEWIDE INTEROPERABLE COMMUNICATIONS GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature accepted a grant from the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services in the amount of $680,557 for the grant period of January 1, 2020 through December 31, 2021 in Resolution #CSS-019-20 on April 21, 2020, and\nWHEREAS, this grant allows the Sheriffs Office to continue the improvement of the interoperable communications network which will have the benefit to the residents of Niagara County of improved reliability of communication for their safety and protection, and\nWHEREAS, an improved interoperable communications network will also enhance interoperable communications with other counties and agencies when participating in Mutual Aid emergencies, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services has extended the grant performance period until December 31,2022,and\nWHEREAS, the grant funds are in the 2022 budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the agreement for the grant extension through December 31, 2022.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-005-22":"Resolution No. CSS-005-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION FY20 STATEWIDE INTEROPERABLE COMMUNICATIONS\nFORMULA GRANTS PROGRAM\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature accepted a grant from the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services in the amount of $755,125 to the Niagara County Sheriffs Office under the New York State FY2020 Statewide Interoperable Communications Formula Grant Program for the grant period of January 1, 2021 through December 31, 2022 in Resolution #CSS-100-21 on October 19, 2021, and\nWHEREAS, this grant will allow the Sheriffs Office to continue the improvement of the interoperable communications network which will have the benefit to the residents of Niagara County of improved reliability of communication for their safety and protection, and\nWHEREAS, an improved interoperable communications network will also enhance interoperable communications with other counties and agencies when participating in Mutual Aid emergencies, and\nWHEREAS, the funds need to be added to the 2022 Budget in order to utilize the grant, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be made,\nINCREASE REVENUE\nA.17.3645.000 43305.02 State Aid, Civil Defense $755,125,\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS\nA.17.3645.000 72100.15 Communications Equipment $755,125\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-006-22":"Resolution No. CSS-006-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFFS OFFICE BUDGET MODIFICATION\nCOMPLEX COORDINATED TERRORISM ATTACK GRANT EXTENSION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed resolution CSS-031-18 on April 17, 2018 to accept a revenue on behalf of the Niagara County Sheriffs Office for $29,700 as part of a Complex Coordinated Terrorism Attack Grant for the performance period of March 1, 2018 through August 31, 2020,and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature also accepted an extension of the grant into 2021 in Resolution #CSS-065-21 on June 12, 2021, and\nWHEREAS, the Complex Coordinated Terrorism Attack Grant is a cooperative grant for the BEN UASI region, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office participation in this grant enhances the preparedness of Niagara County for the safety of its citizens, and\nWHEREAS, the grant has been extended past August 31, 2021 with remaining funds in the grant, and\nWHEREAS, the 2022 budget will need to be modified to allow for these funds to be used, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000.44305.02 Civil Defense Homeland Security $ 10,000\nProject 17GRTCCTA\nFNCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000.71050.00 Overtime Expense 17GRTCCTA $ 8,334\nA.17.3645.000.78100.00 Retirement Expense 17GRTCCTA 815\nA. 17.3645.000.78200.00 FICA Expense 17GRTCCTA 637\nA. 17.3645.000.78300.00 Worker's Compensation Expense 17GRTCCTA 214\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-007-22":"Resolution No. CSS-007-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFFS OFFICE BUDGET MODIFICATION\nACCEPT DOMESTIC CANNABIS ERADICATION/SUPPRESSION PROGRAM\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office wishes to continue an agreement with the U.S. Department of Justice Drug Enforcement Administration for the year 2022, and\nWHEREAS, this agreement allows the Sheriffs Office to work in conjunction with the local DEA Office to curtail illegal cannabis trafficking and in eradication of illicit cannabis plants throughout Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the DEA will reimburse the Sheriffs Office for the costs incurred with this project, now, therefore,\nbe it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County continue an agreement with the U.S. Department of Justice, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the 2022 budget be modified as follows:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA. 17.3110.000.44389.04 Other Public Safety Operation Green Monster $100,000\nINCREASE APPROPmATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000.71050.00 Overtime $68,500\nA.17.3110.000.74400.09 Payments to Other Agencies 15,500\nA.17.3110.000.74600.03 Training/Education 5,000\nA.17.3110.000.74750.05 Law Enforcement Supplies 5,000\nA.17.3110.000.74800.11 Vehicle Maintenance (Helicopter) 6,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-008-22":"Resolution No. CSS-008-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHEmFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - JAIL BODY SCANNER\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Correctional Facility has been greatly affected by the presence of the COVID vims in the community and in the jail, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Jail has revised their operating procedures to include quarantine of incoming incarcerated individuals for 14 days before introduction to the general population, isolation of incarcerated individuals who are ready to transfer to State Prison, and isolation of any incarcerated individuals who test COVID positive, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Jail is consistently looking for new procedures to reduce the risk of the spread ofCOVID-19 within the jail while still maintaining the priority of the safety and security of the incarcerated individuals and staff, and\nWHEREAS, an important preventive measure within jails is to search all incoming individuals for possible contraband whether obvious or hidden which requires close contact with individuals, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Jail has identified a need for a whole body security scanning system which allows for no-contact screening for both metallic and nonmetallic threats including weapons, drugs, cell phone and other contraband with a thermal camera to detect fevers, and\nWHEREAS, the scanner will greatly improve the jail's ability to detect contraband, it will also provide safer scanning process for the jail staff, and\nWHEREAS the County has received stimulus funds to offset COVID related costs in correctional facilities, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara will utilize funds from the Coronavims State and Local Fiscal Recovery Fund (CSLFRF), a part of the America Rescue Plan Act, to fund this equipment, and\nWHEREAS, the 2022 budget will need to be modified to allow for these funds to be used, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following line item transfers be effectuated:\nLNTCREASE REVENUE:\nA.17.3150.000 44089.07 Federal Aid, Other ARPA State/Local Fiscal Rec Fund $159,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3150.000 72100.21 Law Enforcement Equipment $159,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-009-22":"Resolution No. CSS-009-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - COPS OFFICE COMMUNITY POLICING DEVELOPMENT (CPD)\nPROGRAM GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature accepted a COPS Office FY2021 Community Policing\nDevelopment (CPD) Program Grant for the performance period September 1, 2021 through August 31, 2023 in the amount of $200,000 with a match of $84,434 in resolution #CSS-108-21 on November 16, 2021, and\nWHEREAS, the grant funds will be used to ensure that 100% of the officers at the Sheriffs Office and at other Niagara County Law Enforcement Agencies are trained and experienced in de-escalation techniques, and\nWHEREAS, to accomplish this goal the Sheriffs Office will use the grant funds to cover training costs for 2 Train the Trainers for a De-Escalation Course to train the other officers in the county, for overtime for officers to attend the De-Escalation Training Course and to purchase a Virtual Reality Training Simulator VirTra Machine to allow officers to practice the skills and techniques they have learned in the De-Escalation Course, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office will use part of the cost of the Virtual Reality Training Simulator as the match for this grant, and\nWHEREAS, the funds need to be added to the 2022 Budget in order to utilize the grant, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be made:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA. 17.3645.000 44305.02 Civil Defense Homeland Security $200,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS: '\nA.17.3645.000 71050.00 Overtime Expense $ 44,809\nA.17.3645.000 72100.21 Law Enforcement Equipment 100,000\nA.17.3645.000 74400.09 Payments other agencies 54,441\nA.17.3645.000 74600.03 Training and Education 750\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent ~ Elder.","CSS-010-22":"Resolution No. CSS-010-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - STOP DWI FOUNDATION GRANT\nWHEREAS, the New York State Governor's Traffic Safety Committee, STOP-DWI Foundation has notified the Niagara County Sheriffs Office of an additional $3,000 in grant funds available to use for High Visibility Engagement Campaigns this year, and\nWHEREAS, the grant funds are utilized for enforcement efforts by multiple law enforcement agencies during peak holidays with the goal of reducing the number of crashes, injuries and deaths on Niagara County roads, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to modify revenue and appropriations to utilize these funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.17.3315.000.44389.09 Other Public Safety Traffic $3,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3315.000.74400.09 Miscellaneous Expenses Payments Other Agencies $ 3,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent ~- Elder.","CSS-011-22":"Resolution No. CSS-011-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHEmFPS OFFICE\nFEDERAL EQUITABLE SHARING ANNUAL RE CERTIFICATION\nWHEREAS, the United States Department of Justice requires that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office file an annual Federal Equitable Sharing Program Agreement and Certification Report to be eligible to submit requests for federal forfeited property or proceeds, and\nWHEREAS, the Agreement is entered into by the United States Department of Justice, the County of Niagara and the Niagara County Sheriffs Office, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office be authorized to participate in the Federal Equitable Sharing Program, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that prior to the execution of the Federal Equitable Sharing Agreement and Certification, the County Attorney will review the Federal Equitable Sharing Agreement and Certification for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the Annual Equitable Sharing Agreement and Certification.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-012-22":"Resolution No. CSS-012-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHEmFF'S OFFICE\nINTERMUNICIPAL AGREEMENT\nMONROE COUNTY SUBSTITUTE JAIL ORDER\nWHEREAS, the Monroe County Jail contacted the Niagara County Jail in April of 2021 regarding the need for assistance with a substitute jail order for an individual in Monroe County who was deemed to be a safety risk to house in the Monroe County Jail ,and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office agreed to assist the Monroe County Sheriffs Office, and\nWHEREAS, Monroe County agreed to reimburse Niagara County for any overtime hours incurred in the supervision of this inmate at the overtime rate of a Step 10 Correction Officer, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office and Niagara County enter into an Intermunicipal Agreement with Monroe County and the Monroe County Sheriffs Office to be reimbursed from this substitute jail order in 2021, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that prior to the execution of the Intermunicipal Agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney s review, the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the Intermunicipal Agreement.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent ~- Elder.","CSS-013-22":"Resolution No. CSS-013-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security Committee.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHEmFF'S OFFICE\nCOMMITTEE APPROVAL SCAAP GRANT\nWHEREAS, under the State Criminal Alien Assistance Program (SCAAP), the Office of Justice Programs (OJP) of the US Department of Justice (DOJ) makes payments to eligible states and local governments that incur certain types of costs due to incarceration of undocumented criminal aliens during a particular 12 month reporting period, and\nWHEREAS, FY2022 SCAAP applications are now being accepted for the period July 1, 2020 through June 30, 2021,and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has received funding from SCAAP since the year 2000 to offset the cost of correction officer salaries, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office be authorized to participate in the FY2020 SCAAP Program.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-014-22":"Resolution No. CSS-014-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nNIAGARA COUNTY PUBLIC DEFENDER'S OFFICE\nACCEPT OFFICE OF INDIGENT LEGAL SERVICES (OILS)\nDISTRIBUTION #12 (C120029) GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Public Defender's Office has received an award from the New York State Office of Indigent Legal Services (OILS) in the amount of $329,289 for the period of January 1, 2022, through December 31, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, these funds are to be used to improve the quality of legally mandated representation in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the Public Defender's Office will use these funds for continued funding of a Court Assistant position in the Public Defender's Office; this grant will also help fund books and subscriptions, expert services, and transcripts for both the Public Defender's Office and the Conflict Office, funds will also be used for Maintenance in Lieu of Rent (MILOR) for the Conflict Office, and\nWHEREAS, funding from the New York State Office of Indigent Legal Services for the above items is necessary so that the Niagara County Public Defender's Office can continue providing quality services pursuant to Article 18-B of the County Law, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that prior to the execution of the grant, the County Attorney will review the grant for approval to legal form, language and compliance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute this grant.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-015-22":"Resolution No. CSS-015-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nREGIONAL AGREEMENT BETWEEN NIAGARA AND ERIE COUNTY\nTO PERFORM AUTOPSY SERVICES\nWHEREAS, Niagara County and Erie County entered into an agreement in 2002 that allows the Erie County Medical Examiner's Office to perform all of Niagara County's autopsies, which agreement has subsequently been renewed, and\nWHEREAS, the current agreement has expired December 31, 2021, and\nWHEREAS, Erie County has offered us a three-year agreement at $2,100/full autopsy and $l,350/extemal exam, along with other associated fees, said fees increasing by 3.57% for 2023 and 2024, and\nWHEREAS, it is beneficial for Niagara County to continue this practice and promote regionalism and shared services between the counties of Niagara and Erie, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the County of Niagara does hereby agree to the terms and conditions in said agreement and authorizes the Chairman of the Legislature to execute the contract with Erie County and the Erie County Medical Examiner's Office.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CSS-016-22":"Resolution No. CSS-016-22\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nWAIVER OF RESIDENCY REQUIREMENT\nCONFLICT ATTORNEY\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Conflicts Office & Assigned Counsel has permission to fill presently two vacant Conflict Attorney positions, these are important positions that are required to provide legal representation of indigent persons entitled to public defense who cannot be represented by the Public Defender's Office due to a conflict of interest, and\nWHEREAS, due to recent challenges with recruitment efforts of Niagara County residents that are experienced criminal trial attorneys for these vacant positions of Conflict Attorney, and\nWHEREAS, the Conflicts Office & Assigned Counsel has made diligent and sincere efforts to recruit highly qualified candidates for these vacant positions from within Niagara County, including multiple advertisements and postings for an extended period of time, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that a Waiver of Residence from the policy to hire only Niagara County residents be approved to fill these two vacant positions of Conflict Attorney when the most qualified applicant or only qualified candidates resides outside Niagara County.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","CW-001-22":"Resolution No. CW-001-22\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nRESOLUTION AUTHORIZING AND\nAPPROVING A SETTLEMENT AGREEMENT\nTO AN ACTION RELATING TO THE OPIOID CRISIS\n(ALLERGEN, ET AL)\nWHEREAS, there is a pending matter in which Suffolk County, Nassau County and the State of New York entered into a settlement agreement (\"the Agreement\") with AUergan and its subsidiaries regarding the companies manufacturing and distribution ofopioids; and\nWHEREAS, the Agreement is a New York only settlement, with a share of monies being allocated among governmental subdivisions, and\nWHEREAS, political subdivisions in the State, including Niagara County will now have an opportunity to become a party to the Allergen settlement, and\nWHEREAS, the Distributors have offered to settle the claims with a one-time direct payment, half of which may be used in an unrestricted manner and half must be used in a restricted manner on Approved Uses only, and\nWHEREAS, it is m the best interest of the County of Niagara to resolve this matter with respect to the Distributors without further litigation and execute the Allergen New York Subdivision Election and Release Form and Sharing Agreement to participate and receive a share of the Agreement, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the execution and delivery on behalf of and in the name of the County of Niagara by the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature, Rebecca J. Wydysh, or her designee, of the proposed Agreement is hereby authorized, and the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature, Rebecca J. Wydysh, or her designee, is hereby authorized, subject to the approval of the Niagara County Attorney, Claude A. Joerg, and directed to execute the proposed Agreement in a form substantially similar thereto and execute such other documents as may be necessary and appropriate to effectuate the settlement with the Distributors; and be it further\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","ED-001-22":"Resolution No. ED-001-22\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nAPPROVAL OF LOW COST POWER BENEFIT TO\nNIAGARA ENVELOPE, INC. UNDER AGREEMENT FOR THE\nSALE AND PURCHASE OF NIAGARA PROJECT POWER & ENERGY (ASPNPPE)\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara entered into the Host Community Relicensing and Settlement Agreement (\"HCRSA\") on June 25, 2005, and\nWHEREAS, the HCRSA entitles the County of Niagara to 9 megawatts of low cost power allocation (at 70% load) form the New York Power Authority, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has made the use of this low cost power allocation for economic development purposes as the top priority for that allocation, and\nWHEREAS, there is presently an Agreement for the Sale and Purchase of Niagara Project Power and Energy (\"ASPNPPE\") that has been approved by the County of Niagara and NYPA and the Governor of the State of New York, and\nWHEREAS, the ASPNPPE specifically provides that the County of Niagara, could use its low cost power allocation for economic development purposes, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has taken the necessary actions to fully implement the benefits contained in the HCRSA and the ASPNPPE, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara established the Empower Niagara Board by resolution ED-021-07 to review applications for the use of low cost power for economic development by reviewing applications and to make recommendation to Legislature of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has previously awarded to Niagara Envelope, Inc. for this same project under resolution ED-004-19, 225 Kw (at 70% load factor), which agreement expired on December 31, 2021 and for which Niagara Envelope, Inc. has met each part of the previous application, including the number of jobs and capital investment, and\nWHEREAS, the Empower Niagara Board has reviewed the application of Niagara Envelope, Inc. for the use of low cost power so that it can remain competitive in the global market, which would encompass diversifying its project lines and possibly adding a new flexible packing product line in 2022, adding an additional three to five new full-time jobs, together with the addition of a third shift, which would require four new employees, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara Envelope, Inc. has met or exceeded all the criteria qualifying its original project for consideration and the Empower Niagara Board is recommending approval of a three-year extension of the agreement, and\nWHEREAS, the demand usage of Niagara Envelope, Inc. has been reduced from 225 kw of low cost power to 195 kw of low cost power, based on the average usage, and\nWHEREAS, the approval of Niagara County's extension of low cost power will not only maintain economic development in the Town of Wheatfield and Niagara County; but also assist Niagara Envelope, Inc. to remain competitive in the global printing and manufacturing industry and have a good economic effect on the community in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the Empower Niagara board has recommended the extension and decrease of the low cost power allocations of 195kw (at 70% load factor) for a period of three (3) years commencing January 1, 2022, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature hereby approves Niagara Envelope, Inc. agreement extension for three (3) years commencing January 1, 2022 for 195kw (at 70% load factor) low cost power benefits from Niagara County, pursuant to low cost power allocation received through the June 25, 2005 HCRSA and ASPNPPE, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute this Empower User Agreement extension of the agreement for Low Cost Power with Niagara Envelope, Inc.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent ~ Elder.","ED-002-22":"Resolution No. ED-002-22\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nAPPROVAL OF LOW COST POWER BENEFIT TO\nVIRTUAL POLYMER COMPOUNDS, LLC UNDER AGREEMENT FOR THE\nSALE AND PURCHASE OF NIAGARA PROJECT POWER & ENERGY (ASPNPPE)\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara entered into the Host Community ReUcensing and Settlement Agreement (\"HCRSA\") on June 25, 2005, and\nWHEREAS, the HCRSA entitles the County of Niagara to 9 megawatts of low cost power allocation (at 70% load) from the New York Power Authority, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has made the use of this low cost power allocation for economic development purposes as the top priority for that allocation, and\nWHEREAS, there is presently an Agreement for the Sale and Purchase of Niagara Project Power and Energy (\"ASPNPPE\") that has been approved by the County of Niagara and NYPA and the Governor of the State of New York, and\nWHEREAS, the ASPNPPE specifically provides that the County of Niagara, could use its low cost power allocation for economic development purposes, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has taken the necessary actions to fully implement the benefits contained in the HCRSA and the ASPNPPE, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara established the Empower Niagara Board by resolution ED-021-07 to review applications for the use of low cost power for economic development by reviewing applications and to make recommendation to Legislature of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the application of Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC, an original custom fiberglass manufacturing in five unique product lines covering industrial and recreational, with manufacturing facilities in Medina and Lockport, and administrative offices in Getzville, which employs 38 employees, has met and exceeded all the criteria qualifying its project for consideration and the Empower Niagara Board is recommending approval of this application, and\nWHEREAS, the application was approved for 200 kw (at 70% load factor) of low cost power, which will enable Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC to acquire and renovate the 86,000 square foot building at 222 S. Niagara Street, Lockport, and allow Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC to consolidate to one facility and reduce costs and increase efficiency, and\nWHEREAS, the approval of the Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC application for 200 kw (at 70% load factor) of low cost power will not only increase economic development in the City of Lockport and Niagara County, but will also assist Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC to remain competitive in the custom fiberglass manufacturing industry and will also have a good economic effect on the community in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the Empower Niagara Board has recommended the approval of the application for low cost power allocation of 200 kw (at 70% load factor) for a period of three (3) years commencing February 1, 2022, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature hereby approves the application of Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC for 200 kw (at 70% load factor) low cost power benefits from Niagara County, pursuant to low cost power allocation received through the June 25, 2005 HCRSA and ASPNPPE, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute this Empower User Agreement for Low Cost Power with Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","IF-001-22":"Resolution No. IF-001-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nASBESTOS ABATEMENT AND FLOOR PREPARATION-\nCOURT HOUSE AND CIVIC BUILDING\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 7\nWHEREAS, by Resolution No. IF-090-18, dated June 19, 2018, the Legislature awarded the contract for the Asbestos Abatement and Floor Preparation-Court House and Civic Building Project to Metro Environmental, 2939 Lockport Road, Niagara Falls, NY 14305, for a contract amount of $184,000, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-152-18, dated December 4, 2018, authorized Change Order No. 1 to increase the contract in the amount of $9,336 for abatement of additional pipe joint elbow mudded fittings, for a revised contract amount of $193,3336, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-067-19, dated May 21, 2019, authorized the extension of the contract completion date to December 31, 2019, at no additional cost to the County, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-028-20, dated Febmary 18, 2020, authorized Change Order No. 3 to increase the contract in the amount of $2,300 to allow for an additional notification fee required by the New York State Department of Labor due to project extension beyond one (1) year from commencement of the project, for a revised contract amount of $195,636, and to extend the contract completion date to Febmary 18, 2021, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-043-20, dated March 10, 2020, authorized Change Order No. 4, to allow for additional abatement of floor tiles and mastic and drilling for MEP work, in the amount of $17,477.14, for a revised contract amount $213,113.14, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-023-21, dated March 23, 2021, authorized Change Order No. 5 to increase the contract in the amount of $2,300 to allow for an additional notification fee required by the New York State Department of Labor due to a project extension beyond one (1) year from commencement of the project, for a revised contract amount $215,413.14, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-115-21, dated December 14, 2021, authorized Change Order No. 6 to increase the contract in the amount of $13,211.11 for select demolition of non-stmctural partition walls and acoustic tile and grid ceilings in the third floor offices where flooring abatement work is to occur for a revised contract amount of $228,624.25, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to increase the contract in the amount of $5,937.31 for removal of flooring materials additional third floor offices adjacent to the current flooring abatement work is to occur for a revised contract amount of $234,561.56, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 7 to increase the contract by $5,937.31 for the Asbestos Abatement and Floor Preparation-Court House and Civic Building Project, for a revised contract amount of $234,561.56 to Metro Environmental, 2939 Lockport Road, Niagara Falls, NY 14305, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney s review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","IF-002-22":"Resolution No. IF-002-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nBEAR RIDGE ROAD CULVERT REHABILITATION PROJECT\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 3 - FINAL\nWHEREAS, by Resolution No. IF-053-21, dated May 11, 2021, the Legislature awarded the contract for the Bear Ridge Road Culvert Rehabilitation Project to Concrete Applied Technologies Corp., (CATCO), 1266 Townlme Road, Alden, NY 14004, for a contract amount of $227,749, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-096-21, dated September 21, 2021, authorized Change Order No. 1 to the contract to extend the contract completion date to October 1, 2021, at no additional cost to the County, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-117-21, dated December 14, 2021, authorized Change Order No. 2 to increase the contract in the amount of $40,000 to install additional concrete and guide rail, for a revised contract amount of $267,749, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to reduce the contract in the amount of $3,161.25 for final quantities adjustments, for a revised contract amount of $264,587.75, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 3-Final to decrease the contract by $3,161.25 for the Bear Ridge Road Culvert Rehabilitation Project, for a revised contract amount of $264,587.75, to Concrete Applied Technologies Corp., (CATCO), 1266 Townline Road, Alden, NY 14004, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","IF-003-22":"Resolution No. IF-003-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nCULVERT REHABILITATION-PRECISION TRENCHLESS LLC\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 2 - FINAL\nWHEREAS, by Resolution No. IF-075-21, dated June 15, 2021, the Legislature awarded the contract for the Rehabilitation of Six Cross Culverts Project to Precision Trenchless LLC, 1710 Erie Blvd., Schenectady, NY 12308, for a contract amount of $212,650, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-092-21, dated August 3, 2021, authorized Change Order No. 1 to increase the contract in the amount of $2,700 for OCP insurance that is required for the project, for a revised contract amount of $215,350, and <\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to reduce the contract in the amount of $38,716.45 for final quantities adjustments, for a revised contract amount of $176,633.55, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 2-Final to decrease the contract by $38,716.45 for the Rehabilitation of Six Cross Culverts Project, for a revised contract amount of $176,633.55, to Precision Trenchless LLC, 1710 Erie Blvd., Schenectady, NY 12308, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent ~ Elder.","IF-004-22":"Resolution No. IF-004-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18,2022\nSUNSET DRIVE ROAD AND DRAINAGE IMPROVEMENTS\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 2 - FINAL\nWHEREAS, by Resolution No. IF-038-21, dated April 20, 2021, the Legislature awarded the contract for the Sunset Drive Road and Drainage Improvements Project to Union Concrete & Construction, P.O; Box 410, West Seneca, NY 14224, for a contract amount of $1,460,346.50, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-079-21, dated June 15, 2021, authorized Change Order No. 1 to increase the contract in the amount of $100,000 for parking lot restoration at the Niagara County Public Safety Training Facility, for a revised contract amount of $1,560,346.50, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessaryto reduce the contract inthe amount of $465,725.10 for final quantities adjustments, . for a revised contract amount of $1,094,621.40, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 2-Final to decrease the contract by $465,725.10 for the Sunset Drive Road and Drainage Improvements Project, for a revised contract amount of $1,094,621.40, to Union Concrete & Construction, P.O. Box 410, West Seneca, NY 14224, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent -- Elder.","IF-005-22":"Resolution No. IF-005-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nBEAR RIDGE ROAD RESURFACING\nCAMBELL BLVD. TO WEST CANAL ROAD\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 1 - FINAL\nWHEREAS, by Resolution No. IF-073-21, dated June 15, 2021, the Legislature awarded the contract for the Bear Ridge Road Resurfacing Project to Amherst Paving, Inc., 330 Meyer Road, Amherst, NY 14226, for a contract amount of $906,683.96, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to decrease the contract m the amount of $269,626.15 due to final quantities adjustments, for a revised contract amount of $637,057.81, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 1-Final to decrease the contract by $269,626.15 for the Bear Ridge Road Resurfacing Project, for a revised contract amount of $637,057.81, to Amherst Paving, Inc., 330 Meyer Road, Ainherst, NY 14226, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","IF-006-22":"Resolution No. IF-006-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nCARMEN ROAD BRIDGE OVER JOHNSON CREEK\nCONSULTANT AMENDMENT NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-106-20, dated September 15, 2020, authorized the contract for consultant services for the Carmen Road Bridge over Johnson Creek Project to CHA Consulting Inc., 2200 Main Place Tower, Buffalo, NY 14202, for a fee not to exceed $242,000, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to amend the contract for construction administration and construction inspection services in the amount of $199,000, for a revised contract amount of $441,000, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Amendment No. 1 to increase the contract by $199,000 for the Carmen Road Bridge over Johnson Creek Project, for a revised contract amount of $441,000 to CHA Consulting Inc., 2200 Main Place Tower, Buffalo, NY 14202, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","IF-007-22":"Resolution No. IF-007-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nRENEW THE SHARED SERVICES AGREEMENT FOR\nSTORMWATER MANAGEMENT COORDINATOR\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works desires to continue the shared services agreement with the City of Tonawanda to assist the County with the compliance of Phase II Stormwater Regulations, and\nWHEREAS, continuation of the shared services agreement with the City ofTonawanda would be beneficial to both municipalities, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account D.15.5140.000 74650.08, Consultant Services, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the shared services agreement for the implementation of the Phase II Stormwater Regulations be renewed with the City ofTonawanda, 200 Niagara Street, Tonawanda, NY 14150, for a contract amount not to exceed $47,100 for the period March 1, 2022 through February 28, 2023, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","IF-008-22":"Resolution No. IF-008-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nAKRON ROAD PAVEMENT REHABILITATION DYSINGER ROAD TO\nLOCKPORT CITY LINE FEDERAL AID LOCAL PROJECT AGREEMENT\nSUPPLEMENTAL AGREEMENT NO. 2\nWHEREAS, the Akron Road Pavement Rehabilitation (from Dysinger Road to the Lockport City Line) Project, Town of Lockport, Niagara County, is eligible for funding under Title 23 US Code, as amended, that calls for the apportionment of the costs of such program to be borne at the ratio of 80% federal funds and 20% non-federal funds, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara desires to advance the Project by making a commitment of 100% of the non-federal share of the costs of the Engineering (Design I-VI) and Constmction/Constmction Inspection phases of the project, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby approves the above-subject project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby authorizes the County of Niagara to pay in the first instance 100% of the federal and non-federal shares of the cost of the Constmction/Construction Inspection phases of the Project or portions thereof, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to reflect the funding levels set forth by Schedule A in the attached agreement: DECREASE ANTICIPATED REVENUE: H701.15.5112.000 43501.00 Consol Hwy Aid Revenue $205,600 L^CREASE ANTICIATED REVENUE: H701.15.5112.000 43591.00 State Aid Cap Const Hwy Rev $228,000 INCREASE ANTICIPATED APPROPRIATIONS: H701.15.5112.000 72600.01 Infrastructure Roads $22,400\nRESOLVED, that the sum of $1,780,000 is hereby appropriated in account H701.15.5112.000 72600.01 and made available to cover the cost of participation in the above phases of the project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that in the event the amount required to pay the full federal and non-federal shares of the cost of the project's Constmcdon/Constmction Inspection phases exceeds the amount appropriated above, the County of Niagara shall convene its Legislature as soon as possible to appropriate said excess amount immediately upon the notification by the New York State Department of Transportation, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Legislature of the County of Niagara be, and hereby is, authorized to execute all necessary Agreements, certifications, or reimbursement requests for Federal Aid on behalf of the County of Niagara with the New York State Department of Transportation, in connection with the advancement or approval of the Project providing for the administration of the Project and the Municipality's first instance funding of Project costs and permanent funding of the local share of Federal Aid eligible Project costs and all Project costs within appropriations therefor that are not so eligible, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that a certified copy of this Resolution be filed with the New York State Commissioner of Transportation by attaching it to any necessary Agreement in connection with the Project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that this Resolution shall take effect immediately.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","IF-009-22":"Resolution No. IF-009-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nCARMEN ROAD BmDGE OVER JOHNSON CREEK BmDGE REPLACEMENT\nSUPPLEMENTAL AGREEMENT NO. 1\nWHEREAS, the Carmen Road Bridge over Johnson Creek Bridge Replacement Project, town of Hartland, Niagara County, BW 3328940 (the \"Project\"), is eligible for funding under Title 23 US Code, as amended, that calls for the apportionment of the costs of such program to be borne at the ratio of 80% Federal Funds and 20% Non-Federal Funds, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara desires to advance the Project by making a commitment of 100% of the Non-Federal share of the costs of the Engineering (Phases I-VI) and Construction/ Construction Inspection phases of the project, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby approves the above-subject project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby authorizes the County of Niagara to pay in the first instance 100% of the federal and non-federal shares of the cost of the Construction/Inspection phases of the Project or portions thereof, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to reflect the funding levels set forth by Schedule A in the agreement: INCREASE ANTICIPATED REVENUE: H62L 15.5197.000 44597.01 Federal Aid Cap Const Hwy Rev $1,200,000 H621.15.5197.000 43591.00 State Aid Cap Const Hwy Rev $ 30,000 mCREASE ANTICIPATED APPROPRIATIONS: H621.15.5197.000 72600.02 Infrastructure Bridges $1,230,000 and be it further\nRESOLVED, that in the event the amount required to pay the full federal and non-federal shares of the cost of the project's Engineering and Construction/Inspection phases exceeds the amount appropriated above, the County of Niagara shall convene its Legislature as soon as possible to appropriate said excess amount immediately upon the notification by the New York State Department of Transportation, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Legislature of the County of Niagara be, and hereby is, authorized to execute all necessary Agreements, certifications, or reimbursement requests for Federal Aid on behalf of the County of Niagara with the New York State Department of Transportation, in connection with the advancement or approval of the Project providing for the administration of the Project and the Municipality's first instance funding of Project costs and permanent funding of the local share of Federal Aid eligible Project costs and all Project costs within appropriations therefor that are not so eligible, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that a certified copy of this Resolution be filed with the New York State Commissioner of Transportation by attaching it to any necessary Agreement in connection with the Project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney s review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that this Resolution shall take effect immediately.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder.","IF-010-22":"Resolution No. IF-010-22\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities Committee.\nDated: January 18, 2022\nDPW-HIGHWAY\nEXPENDITURE OF COUNTY ROAD FUND MONIES\nWHEREAS, the Commissioner of Public Works recommends the expenditure of County Road Fund monies for maintenance of roads under Section 129 of the Highway Law, as set forth on the attached form, in the sum of $8,855,249, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the necessary documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the recommendation of the Commissioner of Public Works be approved, and the sum of $8,855,249 be appropriated from the County Road Fund for maintenance during the year 2022, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 14 Ayes, 0 Noes, 1 Absent - Elder."};
